  "query_preview": "/MONDAY THROUGH FRIDAY/...\nIssued at 310 AM EDT Sat Sep 19 2026\n\n*** LESS HOT NORTH WITH A FEW STORMS MONDAY ***\n\nThe ridge will begin to break down early next work week as yet \nanother shortwave trough crosses the area Monday evening. This could \nspark a few afternoon and evening storms, which may once again carry \nsome risk for severe weather, though fortunately global models and \ntheir respective ensembles suggest some displacement between the \nbest shear (north) and most optimal instability (south); the Low \nResolution Ensemble Forecast (LREF) system only gives a 10-30% joint \nprobability for > 1000 J/kg MUCAPE and > 30 kt 0-6km bulk shear \nacross western/central Kentucky Monday afternoon (and this may be \ninflated due to timing differences among LREF members). Depending on \ntiming of the trough, cold front, and associated storms, the area \nmay see quite the gradient in temperatures during the afternoon, \nwith some locations (most likely southern KY) topping out in the low-\nmid 90s and others in the upper 70s.\n\n*** FEELING MORE LIKE FALL BY MID WEEK ***\n\nBehind the front, more seasonable temperatures should overtake the \nregion with highs in the 70s north and low to mid 80s south by \nTuesday. Wednesday onward, raw (LREF) and calibrated (NBM) guidance \nagree temperatures are likely to peak each afternoon in the 70s and \ndrop each night into the 50s - near to slightly below normal and \ncertainly much more fall-like. This mild, dry weather is likely to \npersist at least through next weekend as the upper level trough \nslowly drifts from the Great Lakes into the Northeast, keeping our \narea under northerly flow/cool advection.",
